# rsync filter: what crosses from the private client repository into this one
# =============================================================================
#
# This repository is not a mirror. It is a curated snapshot of the Dynadoc
# desktop/web client, which is developed in a private repository and copied
# here one commit at a time. Each sync is a single squashed commit built from a
# filtered tree: the private history is never pushed here, because it contains
# credentials that were committed before this repository existed.
#
# The original snapshot (2026-10-21) was produced by hand and the rule was
# never written down. This file is that rule, reconstructed by diffing the two
# trees and verified to reproduce the published file set exactly.
#
# Note on syntax: rsync does not accept a comment at the end of a rule line --
# the comment would become part of the pattern. Every reason below is therefore
# on its own line, above the rule it explains. Keep it that way.
#
# Last sync
# ---------
#   source repo : the private client repository (not named here on purpose)
#   base commit : f1c020f8  (state this repository was last synced to)
#   synced to   : dc43049f
#
#   Note: this repository was rebuilt from a single clean commit on 2026-10-28
#   and the rebuild dropped this file, so the previous bookkeeping (base
#   4067b17a, synced to ada0488d) no longer describes anything that exists
#   here. The file was recovered from the renamed predecessor repository and
#   its base reset to the rebuild commit. Treat the rebuild as the new origin.
#
# Always record the new base commit here when you sync. Without it the next
# person cannot tell which client changes have already been published, and the
# only way to find out is the tree-diffing exercise that produced this file.
#
# Removed at the a4012ae sync: a third kind of rule, for five files whose
# content was scrubbed here because the client hardcoded the meeting
# transcription endpoint as a bare ws://<address>:9001 while this repository
# read it from VITE_TRANSCRIBE_WS_URL. Those had to be three-way merged on
# every sync, with a published internal address as the failure mode. The client
# now reads the same variable, four of the five files became byte-identical on
# their own, and the rule was deleted rather than carried. If a sync ever has
# to diverge from the client's content again, this is the shape that worked --
# but prefer fixing it upstream, as was done here.
#
# How to run a sync
# -----------------
#   1. Export the client commit from the object store, never from a working
#      tree (a working tree carries untracked local files and .env files):
#
#        git -C <client> archive --format=tar <sha> | tar -x -C /tmp/client
#
#   2. Copy into a branch of this repository:
#
#        rsync -a --delete --filter='merge scripts/client-sync.filter' \
#              /tmp/client/ /path/to/this/worktree/
#
#      --delete propagates client deletions. Excluded paths are NOT deleted on
#      this side (--delete-excluded is deliberately not used), which is what
#      makes the "owned by this repository" section below work: one `-` rule
#      both stops a client file coming in and stops this repository's own
#      version being removed.
#
#   3. Re-run the checks below.
#
#   4. Commit with the hooks disabled:
#
#        git -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null commit ...
#
#      This matters. The lint-staged hook reformats every staged file, and a
#      sync stages hundreds of them. On a normal commit it would rewrite the
#      two locale files -- which carry ~1720 keys and 13 deliberate duplicate
#      keys that a formatter does not preserve -- and reformat source this
#      repository is supposed to be receiving verbatim. The point of a sync is
#      that the published tree matches the client byte for byte: that is what
#      makes the next diff readable and the next sync a copy rather than a
#      reconciliation. The code arriving here was already linted in the client
#      repository.
#
# Checks that must pass before committing
# ---------------------------------------
#   - No internal endpoint survives. Both of these must print nothing:
#       git grep -nE '167\.86\.80\.235|212\.47\.71\.5'
#       git grep -nE 'ws://[0-9]{1,3}(\.[0-9]{1,3}){3}'
#   - No env file came through:
#       git ls-files | grep -E '^\.env($|\.)' | grep -v example
#   - The web build still works. This is what proves nothing the build needs
#     was filtered out, since deploy/web/Dockerfile does `COPY . .` and builds:
#       npm ci --ignore-scripts && npm run build:web
#   - git status shows no deletions you cannot explain.


# =============================================================================
# 1. Owned by this repository -- never overwritten by the client
# =============================================================================
# These files exist to make this repository a public project. Their client
# counterparts are internal-facing and would undo that work.

# Public CI, issue templates and CODEOWNERS. Protecting the whole directory
# also keeps out the client's web-image.yml, which pushes to the private
# owner's container registry and names internal cluster resources.
- /.github/

# Written for outside readers rather than for the team.
- /README.md

# Public contribution process.
- /CONTRIBUTING.md

# Release automation and its state. Owned here: the client has no release-please
# setup, so --delete removed all three on the first scripted sync -- which would
# have broken versioning and thrown away the published changelog.
- /.release-please-config.json
- /.release-please-manifest.json
- /CHANGELOG.md

# Public conduct policy, with the enforcement contact address.
- /CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md

# Public vulnerability disclosure process.
- /SECURITY.md

# Licensing is a decision of this repository.
- /LICENSE

# Third-party licence inventory, maintained here.
- /NOTICE

# Provenance of this repository's own history.
- /NOTICE-HISTORY.md

# Public ignore rules; stricter about env files than the client's.
- /.gitignore

# Documents the build variables with no real values in them.
- /.env.example

# This file. It does not exist in the client, so without this rule --delete
# would remove the very rule being applied.
- /scripts/client-sync.filter


# =============================================================================
# 2. Secrets and local configuration
# =============================================================================
# These are tracked in the client repository, which is a problem logged against
# it. They must never reach a public tree.

# Real API and websocket endpoints.
- /.env

# Real development endpoints.
- /.env.development

# Real production endpoints.
- /.env.production


# =============================================================================
# 3. Bundled binaries and build output
# =============================================================================

# ~2.7k files: LibreOffice, a bundled JRE, LanguageTool and other vendored
# runtimes shipped with the desktop build. Fetched at package time, not source.
- /resources/bin/

# .NET intermediate build output of the DocxToHtml converter. Its C# sources
# are published.
- /bin/DocxToHtml/obj/

# .NET publish output of the same converter.
- /bin/DocxToHtml/publish/

# Committed Gradle wrapper binary; regenerated by `gradle wrapper`.
- /android/gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar

# Insurance only. The documented procedure exports with `git archive`, so these
# cannot appear; they would if someone rsynced a working tree by mistake.
# No trailing slash on .git: in a git worktree it is a file, not a directory,
# and a directory-only rule lets --delete destroy the worktree.
- /.git
- /node_modules/
- /out/
- /dist/
- /web/dist/


# =============================================================================
# 4. Internal screenshots
# =============================================================================

# Screenshots of competitors' products, captured for UI comparison. Not ours to
# redistribute.
- /screenshots-similar-apps/

# Dated screenshots of work in progress, kept as an internal changelog.
- /screenshots-app-progress/


